+
+### Build-and-fix loop
+
+When the model gives its final answer in a turn where `write_file`,
+`edit` or `bash` succeeded, acp-bridge builds the project in the session
+cwd before ending the turn. The build is reported to the Client as an
+`execute` tool call titled `Build: <command>` (`rawInput.command` is the
+full command; `completed` / `failed` with the errors as text content).
+
+- **Build fails:** the errors go back to the model as a user message
+  ("The build failed with these errors. Fix their root causes …") and the
+  turn continues with a fresh round budget. The premature answer is shown
+  as a thought, not as the reply.
+- **Build passes:** `Build succeeded.` is appended to the final message.
+- **Still failing after `LLM_VERIFY_MAX_ATTEMPTS` builds** (or the model
+  stops changing files): the turn ends and the final message states that
+  the build still fails, with the remaining errors. A timeout or a build
+  tool that cannot run is reported the same way; success is never claimed.
+
+`LLM_VERIFY=auto` detects the build from the cwd (first match, cached per
+session once found):
+
+| Found in cwd | Build command |
+|--------------|---------------|
+| `*.xcworkspace` / `*.xcodeproj` | `xcodebuild -workspace/-project … -scheme <S> -destination <D> -derivedDataPath ~/Library/Caches/acp-bridge/DerivedData/<name>-<hash of cwd> -disableAutomaticPackageResolution CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO -quiet build`. The scheme comes from `xcodebuild -list -json` (the one named like the project, else like a target, else the first); the destination from `xcodebuild -showBuildSettings -json` (`SUPPORTED_PLATFORMS` / `SDKROOT`): `generic/platform=iOS Simulator`, `platform=macOS`, or the tvOS / watchOS / visionOS simulator. DerivedData never lives inside the project. |
+| `Package.swift` | `swift build --disable-automatic-resolution` |
+| `Cargo.toml` | `cargo check --offline --message-format short` |
+| `gradlew` | `./gradlew --offline compileDebugSources` (Android) or `./gradlew --offline classes` (JVM) |
+| `package.json` / `tsconfig.json` | `npm run build --if-present` when there is a `build` script, else `npx --no-install tsc --noEmit` when there is a `tsconfig.json`; plain JavaScript without either is not built |
+| `go.mod` | `go build ./...` with `GOFLAGS=-mod=mod GOPROXY=off` |
+| anything else | no build |
+
+None of these fetch dependencies, so the loop adds no network traffic. The
+error report keeps only compiler error lines (`file:line:col: error:`,
+`error[E…]`, `error TS…`, Kotlin `e: `, Go `file.go:line:col:`),
+deduplicated, with cwd-relative paths, at most 40 lines / 4000 characters
+(the last lines of output when nothing looks like an error).
+
+### Recommended system prompt (Xcode)
+
+The tools a session gets change with what is connected (`docs_search` only
+with Xcode's tools, `web_search` only while the search server answers,
+`notes` unless `LLM_NOTES=0`), so the prompt refers to them conditionally
+("if you have …"); the specifics live in the tool descriptions. It is
+static for the session, so the backend's cached prompt prefix stays valid.
+Lines used with gemma4:12b in Xcode (a full config is in the repo that
+ships this patch):
+
+```
+Xcode's message may tell you to use "xcode-tools". Only the tools in your tool list exist; never call another tool name.
+Fixing a compile error: the error, file and line are in the message. Read that file once if needed, then edit it. Never search the project for Apple SDK types or members (SKView, SKScene, UIView ...): they are not declared in the project.
+If you are unsure how an Apple API works and you have docs_search, call it once with the type and member names (e.g. "SKView presentScene"), then make the change.
+Don't build the project yourself: after your final answer the project is built automatically and any errors are sent back to you to fix.
+If you have web_search: use it only for public facts Apple's documentation doesn't cover (third-party libraries, a generic error message), with at most 12 generic words. Never include code, file names, paths, or names from this project.
+If you have the notes tool, record the goal and key findings (file:line) on long tasks.
+```
+
+While the build-and-fix loop is on, the `bash` description also says not
+to build with it. The built-in tool schemas are kept short (they are sent
+with every request): system prompt + all schemas ≈ 2,100 tokens by the
+conservative estimate.
+
+### No-progress guard
+
+A small model that lacks the knowledge for a fix tends to read and search
+the project instead of acting (a recorded Xcode session: 21 searches and
+reads, mostly the same `search_code("scene")`, zero edits in 16 minutes).
+The guard watches each turn's tool calls:
+
+- **Duplicates.** An identical read-only call (`read_file`, `list_dir`,
+  `search_code`, `git_status` / `git_diff` / `git_log`, `docs_search`,
+  `web_search`, and `bash` commands that only read, such as `ls`, `cat`,
+  `grep`, `git log`, bare `git branch`; not `sort -o`, `uniq IN OUT`,
+  `tree -o`, `git branch -D`, `git diff --output`, `find -fprint`, `rg
+  --pre`, which write or run something) is not run again: the model gets
+  `[Duplicate: identical to your call in round k …]` plus the first 300
+  characters while the original is at most 4 rounds back, the full result
+  after that. A `write_file` with content the file already has, or an `edit`
+  that was already applied, is reported instead of repeated. Any successful
+  change clears this cache.
+- **Investigation streak.** Read-only calls since the last successful
+  change (a duplicate counts 2). At `LLM_INVESTIGATE_LIMIT` (6) a nudge is
+  appended to the tool result ("Stop investigating … make the most likely
+  fix now"), at 10 a user message asks for an edit or the final answer, from
+  14 read-only calls are refused ("Not run: investigation limit …"), and
+  after three refusals the turn ends with "I could not settle on a fix.
+  What I found:" and a list of the reads and searches. A failed verify build
+  restarts the streak at limit - 3, so the model can read the new errors'
+  files.
+- **SDK-symbol hint.** A `search_code` for a type the project does not
+  declare (`class SKView`, `SKView`) or for a member named in the turn's
+  compiler error (`'scene'`) gets a note that it is an Apple SDK symbol
+  (framework from the name prefix and the project's `import`s; the
+  member's owner type read from the matching line, e.g. `let skView =
+  SKView(…)`) and how to look it up: `docs_search` when Xcode's tools are
+  connected, else `web_search`, else "use your knowledge of SKView's API".
+
+Every guarded call still shows up in the client as a normal tool call with
+the text the model got. The thresholds are logged at `info`.
+
+### Working memory (notes)
+
+Each session has notes in two parts: the model's own (the `notes` tool:
+`{action: read|append|replace, text}`, at most 2,000 characters, 400 per
+append, 3 calls per turn, neutral for the no-progress guard) and an
+automatic part: when the context budget drops old rounds, each newly
+dropped range is summarized once from its tool calls, without an LLM call
+(`r7: read GameView.swift; search "scene" (4 hits: GameView.swift:44, …);
+edit MyApp/GameView.swift OK; build FAILED: …`; at most 2,000 characters,
+oldest collapsed). Every request then ends with
+
+```
+<working-notes>
+<the model's notes, first 1,000 characters>
+Older rounds were removed to fit the context window; their summary is under "Dropped context".
+Dropped context:
+- <newest summaries, last 600 characters>
+</working-notes>
+```
+
+appended to the LAST message of the request copy (never to the system
+prompt, so the cached prefix stays valid; the stored history never contains
+it). Room for the block is kept in the context budget. When the guard ends
+a turn, the model's notes are part of the summary. With `LLM_NOTES=0` there
+is no tool and no block; a dropped-rounds note is still added at the end.
+
+### Session persistence and `session/resume`
+
+With a state directory (`[persist] state_dir`, or `LLM_STATE_DIR`) each
+session is stored under `<state_dir>/sessions/<session id>/`:
+
+| File | Contents |
+|------|----------|
+| `meta.json` | id, cwd, title (first line of the first prompt), created / updated time, model, protocol version, web searches made |
+| `history.jsonl` | the conversation as sent to the model, one message per line, appended and flushed as the turn goes (user message, each tool call with its results, nudges, the reply). No system prompt: a resumed session gets the one from the current config. Images are stored as `[image omitted]`. |
+| `NOTES.md` | the [working notes](#working-memory-notes) (model part and summaries of dropped rounds) |
+| `.lock` | pid of the acp-bridge process that has the session open (stale once that pid is gone, or when the process now using the pid started after the lock was written) |
+
+Directories are created `0700` and files `0600`. Every string is redacted
+before it is written: the values of `LLM_API_KEY`, `BRAVE_API_KEY` and any
+`*_KEY` / `*_TOKEN` / `*_SECRET` / `*PASSWORD*` environment variable of 12
+or more characters, PEM private-key blocks, and tokens starting with `sk-`,
+`ghp_`, `gho_`, `ghs_`, `github_pat_`, `xox?-`, `AKIA` or `AIza`. What is
+stored is what the model saw: prompts (including what the editor injects),
+replies, tool arguments and results, i.e. excerpts of the project's
+source. Past 20 MB of history, new tool results are stored as a
+placeholder. A disk error is logged and never fails a turn.
+
+With persistence on, `initialize` advertises (v1)
+`agentCapabilities.sessionCapabilities: {resume: {}, close: {}, list: {}}`
+(not `loadSession`: there is no history replay). Xcode re-attaches a stored
+conversation with `session/resume` when this is advertised; it keeps its
+own transcript. v2's `capabilities.session` already means
+new / list / resume / close.
+
+- `session/resume {sessionId, cwd, mcpServers}`: loads the session from
+  disk (or reuses it when it is still in memory), answers `{}`, then sends
+  the slash commands and the stored title. The system prompt comes from the
+  current config and the cwd from the request; tool calls left unanswered
+  by a crash get "Not run: the session was interrupted.". The request's MCP
+  servers are connected as for `session/new` (a live session keeps a
+  working connection to the same server and replaces a different one).
+  Errors: `-32602` for an id acp-bridge could not have minted (not a UUID,
+  e.g. `../x`) or a `replayFrom` (v2), `-32002` with `data.reason:
+  "unknown_session"` for an unknown id, `-32005` (`session_busy`) while a
+  turn runs or another acp-bridge process has the session.
+- `session/list {cwd?, cursor?}`: stored sessions that have at least one
+  message, newest first, 50 per page (`nextCursor`).
+- `session/close` / `session/end`: frees the session in memory; the stored
+  copy stays (unless `retention_days = 0`).
+- `session/prompt` for a session evicted by `LLM_SESSION_IDLE_TIMEOUT`
+  restores it (and reconnects its MCP servers) first.
+- `session/load` answers `-32601` (`load_not_supported`).
+
+Retention runs at startup and then daily: sessions older than
+`retention_days` (default 30), sessions that never got a message after a
+day, with `retention_days = 0` sessions left behind without
+`session/close` (a crash) after a day, and beyond 200 sessions or 500 MB
+the oldest. A session locked by a running process is never deleted. Without a state directory, or with
+`LLM_PERSIST=0`, nothing is written and `session/load` / `session/resume`
+answer `-32001` (`no_persistence`) as before.
+
+### Reasoning-loop check
+
+gemma4's thinking sometimes repeats the same sentences until `max_tokens`
+(a recorded Xcode reply repeated two sentences for 8 minutes); Ollama only
+stops one token repeated 100 times. After every reply acp-bridge discards
+it when it ran into `max_tokens` (`finish_reason: length` / `done_reason`),
+or, for a reply without a tool call, when its reasoning or text loops: a
+natural-language sentence occurs 3+ times in the last 4,000 characters and
+repeated sentences cover 40%+ of the last 1,500 (code drafts are not
+sentences), an unpunctuated tail repeats 3+ times, or the reasoning is
+longer than `LLM_MAX_REASONING_CHARS`. The discarded reply never enters the
+history; the client sees one thought "(stopped a repeating line of
+reasoning; retrying)", the model gets the user message "You were going in
+circles. Do not reconsider: pick the most likely option and act now with
+ONE tool call, or give your final answer." (plus "split large files" after
+a `length` stop), and that one retry is sent with thinking off
+(`reasoning_effort: "none"` on `/v1`, `think: false` on Ollama's native
+API). A second loop in the same turn ends it as failed. The loop still costs
+one full generation before it is caught (non-streaming requests).
+
+### Context budget
+
+Before every LLM request acp-bridge estimates its size (system prompt,
+history and tool schemas at 3.5 characters per token) against
+`LLM_MODEL_CONTEXT - LLM_RESERVE_OUTPUT_TOKENS - 1024`, where the reserve
+defaults to min(`LLM_MAX_TOKENS`, 4096) (`[llm] reserve_output_tokens` in
+the config file). With a 16384-token context and `max_tokens = 8192` that
+is 11264 prompt tokens instead of 7168. A reply that would not fit the rest
+of the window ends early with `finish_reason: length`, which the
+[reasoning-loop check](#reasoning-loop-check) handles. When the
+request is too large a copy of the history is shrunk, oldest first, until
+it fits: file contents in old `write_file` / `edit` arguments are replaced
+by `[contents of <path> elided - call read_file for the current version]`
+(and old reasoning text is dropped), then old tool results are cut to 400
+characters, then whole old rounds are dropped (a tool-call message always
+together with its results, so no call or result is orphaned). The system
+prompt, the turn's user message and the latest user message are never
+touched (the system message stays byte-identical, so the backend's cached
+prompt prefix stays valid), and the stored session history is not
+modified. When rounds were dropped, the end of the request says so (see
+[working memory](#working-memory-notes)). Set
+`LLM_MODEL_CONTEXT` to the context the backend really uses (e.g. Ollama's
+`num_ctx`).
 
