+### MCP client
+
+acp-bridge has a small built-in MCP client (protocol 2025-06-18; it also
+accepts servers answering 2025-03-26 or 2024-11-05), hand-written on the
+crates it already uses so every connection goes through one audited place:
+
+- **stdio** servers (the editor's, e.g. Xcode's `mcpbridge`) run in their
+  own process group and are killed with their session. Their environment
+  starts empty: only `PATH`, `HOME`, `USER`, `LOGNAME`, `TMPDIR`, `LANG`,
+  `LC_*` plus what the editor's server entry sets, so `LLM_API_KEY` and
+  friends never reach them. A server's `ping` is answered, other server
+  requests are refused.
+- **Streamable HTTP** servers must be on `127.0.0.1`, `::1` or
+  `localhost`; no proxy, no redirects; JSON or SSE responses;
+  `Mcp-Session-Id` kept and re-initialized once after a 404.
+- Calls carry a progress token; a cancelled or timed-out call sends
+  `notifications/cancelled`.
+
+`cargo run --release --example mcp_probe -- http://127.0.0.1:8765/mcp`
+does a read-only handshake + `tools/list` against a local server (no tool
+calls). How the tools reach the model is described under [Xcode
+tools](#xcode-tools-mcp) and [web search](#web-search).
+
+### Xcode tools (MCP)
+
+Xcode 27 passes its tool server in `session/new`: `{name: "xcode-tools",
+command: "/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer/usr/bin/mcpbridge",
+env: [MCP_XCODE_PID, MCP_XCODE_SESSION_ID]}`. When the command is in
+`[mcp] allowed_commands` (canonical paths; default: Xcode and Xcode-beta's
+mcpbridge) acp-bridge starts exactly that command with that environment
+(never `xcrun mcpbridge`, which may pick another Xcode). Anything else the
+editor passes is not started (logged at `warn`); HTTP entries only with
+`allow_client_http = true` and a loopback URL. `initialize` advertises
+`mcpCapabilities {http: false, sse: false}` (v1) / `session.mcp {stdio:
+{}}` (v2).
+
+- **Background connection.** `session/new` answers at once. Xcode shows
+  "Allow acp-bridge to access Xcode?" the first time per Xcode launch (and
+  again after acp-bridge's binary changes); the handshake waits for that.
+  The first prompt waits up to `connect_timeout_secs` (30); if the server
+  is still connecting the turn runs without its tools (one note in the
+  thoughts) and they appear from the next turn. A failure ("Xcode MCP
+  access is disabled; turn it on in Xcode Settings > Intelligence > Model
+  Context Protocol …") is logged once and shown once as a thought. The tool
+  list never changes inside a turn. Closing (or evicting) the session kills
+  the server.
+- **What the model sees.** Only tools in `[mcp.tools."xcode-tools"]
+  expose` (default: `DocumentationSearch`), and never Xcode's own long
+  schemas: DocumentationSearch is offered as `docs_search {query,
+  frameworks?}` (~70 tokens: "Search Apple's developer documentation on
+  this Mac (offline) …"). The result is the top 3 documents as `title (uri)`
+  plus contents (1,000 characters each, 3,500 in total); the client row is
+  titled `mcp__xcode-tools__DocumentationSearch` (kind `search`, `rawOutput
+  {query, documents: [{title, uri}]}`). At most 4 searches per turn;
+  duplicates are answered by the guard; the guard's SDK hint names it.
+  Other exposed tools appear as `mcp__xcode-tools__<Tool>` with their schema
+  minus Xcode's workspace parameters. `allow` lists what acp-bridge itself
+  may call (diagnostics, builds, file lookup); Xcode's crash and field
+  performance tools (data from Apple's servers), `XcodeMessageSend` and any
+  open-world tool are refused in code whatever the config says.
+- **Workspace ids.** Xcode 26.x tools take `tabIdentifier`, 27 GA tools
+  `workspaceIdentifier`. When a call fails with Xcode's "… is required for
+  this action. Choose from the following open workspaces: * …: X,
+  workspacePath: P", acp-bridge picks the entry whose path is the session's
+  project, injects it, retries once and reuses it for the session.
+- **Cancel.** A cancelled turn sends MCP `notifications/cancelled` for a
+  running Xcode call.
+- **Diagnostics after Swift edits** (no schema cost). After a successful
+  `write_file` / `edit` of a `.swift` file, acp-bridge maps the file to
+  Xcode's navigator path (XcodeGlob `**/<name>`, the one candidate whose
+  path ends with the file's relative path; cached; skipped when none or
+  several match) and calls XcodeRefreshCodeIssuesInFile (limit
+  `LLM_XCODE_DIAG_TIMEOUT_SECS`, default 10). The edit's result then ends
+  with `[Xcode diagnostics for GameView.swift: 1 error]` + up to 5 `L45:
+  message` lines (600 characters), or `[Xcode diagnostics: no errors]`, so
+  a wrong fix shows up in the same round. Only error lines count
+  (`path:45:16: error: …`, `[error] …`, `error: …`; `fatal error` too):
+  warnings and notes, also ones that quote a variable named `error`, and
+  summary lines do not; `diagnosticsCount: 0` means no errors; a result in
+  a format that is not recognized adds nothing. A slow or failed call adds
+  nothing; the edit never fails because of it. Needs `XcodeGlob` and
+  `XcodeRefreshCodeIssuesInFile` in `allow` (the default).
+- **Builds in Xcode.** With `[verify] command = "auto"`, an
+  `.xcodeproj` / `.xcworkspace` in the cwd and xcode-tools connected
+  (BuildProject in `allow`), the [build-and-fix loop](#build-and-fix-loop)
+  builds with Xcode's BuildProject instead of a second `xcodebuild`: no
+  competing build, Xcode's own DerivedData, and the build shows in Xcode.
+  The row is titled `Build: Xcode (BuildProject)`; Xcode's progress
+  notifications become `Building… n/m` updates; cancel sends
+  `notifications/cancelled`. Errors (`errors[{filePath, lineNumber,
+  message, type}]`, errors only, at most 15) go back as `path:line: error:
+  message`. "Cancelled by a user interaction in Xcode" ends the turn with
+  "The build was cancelled in Xcode; how should I proceed?". If BuildProject
+  is missing, fails to start or times out (`[verify] timeout_secs`), the
+  reason is logged and the `xcodebuild` build runs instead. A model call of
+  `BuildProject` runs this build at once (counted in
+  `LLM_VERIFY_MAX_ATTEMPTS`). An explicit `[verify] command` is always run
+  as given. Network: this is Xcode's own build, so when the project's
+  package dependencies changed Xcode may resolve and fetch Swift packages
+  (as it would for a build started in Xcode); the `xcodebuild` fallback
+  never does (`-disableAutomaticPackageResolution`).
+- **Tool list changes.** When Xcode sends
+  `notifications/tools/list_changed` (for example after a workspace
+  finished loading), the next turn lists the tools again (5 s limit; on
+  failure the previous list stays).
+- **Commands.** Only commands in `[mcp] allowed_commands` are started, and
+  `mcpbridge` only as an MCP server: a subcommand argument such as
+  `run-agent <agent>` is refused (option flags pass).
+
+### Web search
+
+With a `[[mcp.servers]]` entry `role = "web_search"` pointing at the local
+Brave MCP server (`url = "http://127.0.0.1:8765/mcp"`, `allow =
+["brave_web_search"]`; loopback URLs only, the search key stays with that
+server), the model gets one slim tool, `web_search(query)` (~110 tokens).
+The server is connected lazily at a turn boundary (2 s handshake); when it
+does not answer there is simply no `web_search` that turn, and it is
+re-checked at most every 5 minutes. Each call maps to `brave_web_search`
+with fixed arguments `{query, count: 5, result_filter: ["web"],
+text_decorations: false}` (nothing else the model passes is forwarded);
+the model gets up to 5 lines `title — description (url)` (1,500
+characters) and "(Pages cannot be opened; use these snippets.)". The row is
+titled `Web search: <query>` (kind `fetch`, `rawOutput {query,
+documents}`).
+
+**Privacy guard (in code).** Before anything leaves the machine the query
+is checked and refused (never silently rewritten; the model gets the reason
+and may rewrite once) when it has: control characters or any non-ASCII
+character (zero-width, bidi and other format characters, full-width or
+look-alike letters and punctuation that search engines fold back to
+ASCII), more than 150 characters or 12 words; code syntax (`{ } ; = < > [ ] $ | \`, `->`, `::`,
+`//`, `&&`, `import X`, `let x:` …; parentheses only as a selector like
+`presentScene(_:)`); a path or a file name (`/`, `~`, `.swift`, `.plist`
+…); a URL, e-mail address, IP, `localhost` or an internal host name
+(`site:<public domain>` is fine); something that looks like a key or an id
+(long mixed tokens, hex, UUIDs, `sk-` / `ghp_` / `AKIA` … prefixes, the
+values of `*_KEY` / `*_TOKEN` / `*_SECRET` variables in the environment); or
+a name from the workspace: file and folder names, project / workspace
+names, bundle id and organisation parts from `project.pbxproj`, the git
+remote's owner and repo, the account name (`/Users/<name>/…`, `$USER`), and
+type and function names declared in the sources, also written as separate
+words (`Game Scene` for `GameScene`). The names are collected from at most
+3,000 files, again after every tool call that may have written files (so a
+type the model just declared is refused in its next query), when a resume
+moves the session to another cwd, and at the latest every 10 minutes;
+generic words such as `scene` or `view` and `let`/`var` names are not
+collected. Type names shorter than 8 characters are matched with their
+case (`Kraken`, not `kraken`), so common words stay usable.
+Budgets: 3 searches per turn, 20 per session (stored with the session, so
+a restart and `session/resume` do not reset it); a repeated query is
+answered from the session cache. Every query that goes out is logged at `info`
+(`web_search query=…`), every refusal at `warn` with its rule. The guard is
+a heuristic: a project string that is not a declared name can still pass;
+the 12-word cap and the log limit the damage.
+
+### Tool-call rows (`rawOutput`)
+
+Every finished tool call (`tool_call_update` with `status`) also carries
+the standard optional `rawOutput` with the result in the shape of Xcode's
+native tool rows: `search_code` -> `{pattern, path, results: ["file:line:
+text", ≤50], matchCount}`, `read_file` -> `{filePath, content (≤16 KB),
+lineCount}`, `list_dir` -> `{path, items}`, `bash` / `git_*` -> `{command,
+output (≤8 KB)}`, the verify build -> `{command, output}`. `write_file` /
+`edit` keep showing their diff and send none. Long-running calls (Xcode
+builds) also send `tool_call_update`s with only new `content` as progress.
+
